---
import { apps, byName, getSite, HUB_SLUG, type StorageKind } from '@shkriuss/registry';
import { AppShell } from '@shkriuss/ui';

// The table is built from the registry, so it is always up to date.
const sites = [getSite(HUB_SLUG), ...[...apps].sort(byName).map((app) => getSite(app.slug))];
const pushSites = sites.filter((site) => site.pushNotifications);
const syncSites = sites.filter((site) => site.sync);
const librarySites = sites.filter((site) => site.library);
/** "Calendar, Notes and Tally" */
const listOf = (names: string[]) =>
  new Intl.ListFormat('en-GB', { type: 'conjunction' }).format(names);

const whereLabel: Record<StorageKind, string> = {
  none: 'Nothing is stored',
  device: 'Only in your browser, on your device',
  server: 'On a server (explained in the app)',
};

const LAST_UPDATED = '23 October 2026';
---

<AppShell
  title="Privacy"
  description="What shkriuss.app apps store, where, and what they never do."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="muted">Last updated: {LAST_UPDATED}</p>

    <p class="lead">
      Short version: these apps don't collect your personal data. There are no cookies, analytics,
      ads or trackers, and you don't need an account. What you save in an app stays in your browser
      on your device, unless you turn on an option that clearly says otherwise (like the optional
      account, or reminders while an app is closed, below).
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-is-stored">What each app stores</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-labelledby="what-is-stored" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Site</th>
            <th scope="col">What it saves</th>
            <th scope="col">Where</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {sites.map((site) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{site.name}</th>
              <td>
                {site.dataStored.length > 0 ? (
                  <ul>
                    {site.dataStored.map((item) => (
                      <li>{item}</li>
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                ) : (
                  'Nothing'
                )}
              </td>
              <td>
                {whereLabel[site.storage]}
                {site.sync && ' (and, if you turn on sync, a locked copy online)'}
                {site.pushNotifications &&
                  ' (and, if you turn on reminders while it’s closed, your reminders, locked, on its server)'}
                {site.slug === HUB_SLUG &&
                  ' (and, if you add a passkey, your account key, locked, on its server)'}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    {pushSites.length > 0 && (
      <>
        <h2 id="closed-notifications">Notifications while an app is closed (optional)</h2>
        <p>
          {listOf(pushSites.map((site) => site.name))} can remind you even when the app is closed.
          This is off until you turn it on, separately in each app and on each device. Phones only
          wake closed web apps for messages from a server, so when it's on:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Your device locks each reminder so that only that device can read it, then sends it to
            the app's own server on Cloudflare. Neither the server nor the push service that
            delivers it (Google, Apple, Mozilla, or a UnifiedPush server you chose) can read it.
          </li>
          <li>
            The server stores, for your device: the delivery address your browser's push service
            gave it, your time zone, and for each reminder when it's due and how it repeats. It
            never gets what the reminders are about.
          </li>
          <li>
            Each reminder that won't happen again is deleted after it's sent. Turning the option
            off, or deleting all data in the app, deletes everything for that device. If the server
            can't be reached then, the app says so and tries again each time it's opened.
          </li>
          <li>
            Blocking notifications deletes everything for that device the next time you open the
            app, or when a reminder is due and the push service says the device no longer takes
            them. The server also deletes everything for a device when none of its reminders can be
            delivered for two days.
          </li>
          <li>
            The app sends your reminders again each week while you use it. If you don't open the app
            on that device for 13 months, the server deletes everything it has for that device.
          </li>
          <li>
            The push service sees when each reminder is sent, which app sends it, and roughly how
            long it is, but not what it says. It already knows which phone or browser it delivers
            to, as it does for any app's notifications.
          </li>
          <li>Nothing is shared between apps, and the server is never told who you are.</li>
        </ul>
      </>
    )}

    <h2 id="account">Your shkriuss account (optional)</h2>
    <p>
      You can create a shkriuss account on the <a href="/account/">Account page</a>. It needs no
      email, password or name: the account is a random key made on your device, and a recovery key
      that you keep.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>On your device:</strong> the account key, and which apps you turned on sync for on
        that device, in your browser's storage for shkriuss.app only. They're never in backups.
        Signing out deletes them from that device.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>If you add a passkey:</strong> the account's own server on Cloudflare keeps your
        account key locked so that only that passkey can unlock it. The server can't unlock it and
        doesn't know whose it is. Removing the passkey deletes it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>When you add a device with a code:</strong> for at most 10 minutes, the server keeps
        your account key locked with a one-time key that's only in the QR code. It's deleted as soon
        as your other device uses it, or you tap Done.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>If you replace your account key</strong> (after losing a device): the server keeps a
        note that the old key was replaced, so devices that still have it sign out, with a tag that
        only a device with both the old and the new key can check. The note is deleted once no
        device has asked about it for 13 months. Your device keeps the old key until you sign out
        (or sign in to another account), so your apps can move their synced data to the new one.
      </li>
      <li>
        That's all. There's no list of accounts, and nothing stored that connects an account to you,
        to your devices, or to your data in any app. Like any web request, each request comes with
        your device's internet address and the time: Cloudflare's servers handle them to answer it,
        and nothing here keeps them.
      </li>
    </ul>

    {syncSites.length > 0 && (
      <>
        <h2 id="sync">Syncing an app between your devices (optional)</h2>
        <p>
          {listOf(syncSites.map((site) => site.name))} can keep their data the same on your devices
          through your account. It's off until you tap “Turn on sync” in the app, on each device.
          When it's on:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            The app gets its own key from your account (never the account key itself), and locks its
            data with it on your device before sending it. Only your devices can unlock it.
          </li>
          <li>
            The app's own server on Cloudflare keeps one locked copy of the app's data. It sees how
            big the copy is and when it changes, but never what's in it. Nothing it stores says
            which account is yours, or links one app's copy to another's (each sync comes with your
            internet address and the time, which aren't kept).
          </li>
          <li>
            A copy that no device has synced with for 13 months is deleted, photos and all. Your
            devices keep their data, and the next sync from one of them puts it online again. So
            that the storage all the apps share can't fill up, the server also keeps how much room
            each copy takes.
          </li>
          <li>
            In apps with photos, each photo is locked on your device in the same way and kept with
            the copy. The server sees how many there are and how big, never what they show. Deleting
            a photo deletes it online too.
          </li>
          {librarySites.length > 0 && (
            <li>
              {listOf(librarySites.map((site) => site.name))} also keeps your whole library online,
              as the original files. Each photo is locked on your device before it's sent, and kept
              in Cloudflare's file storage (R2). The server sees how many photos there are, how big,
              and when they're added or deleted, never what they show, their names or their dates.
              Adding photos needs a storage code that only the owner of these apps has, which keeps
              the storage bill under control. When someone types a wrong code, a scrambled form of
              their internet address is kept for up to an hour, to limit guessing. Deleting a photo
              deletes it online and on your other devices.
            </li>
          )}
          <li>
            Settings that belong to one device (like notifications and your theme) don't sync.
          </li>
          <li>
            “Turn off on this device” stops syncing there and keeps your data. “Delete the online
            copy” deletes the copy (and its photos, and any online library) from the server; your
            devices keep what they have. A small note stays, so your other devices learn it was
            deleted: when, and how many times it was changed, never what was in it. It's deleted too
            once no device has asked about it for 13 months.
          </li>
          <li>
            If you replace your account key, turning on sync again moves the app's copy to the new
            key and deletes the old copy.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </>
    )}

    <h2>What these apps never do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Set cookies.</li>
      <li>
        Use analytics, ads, tracking pixels, or anything loaded from another company (scripts, fonts
        or embeds).
      </li>
      <li>Send anything off your device unless you turn on an option that says it does.</li>
      <li>Make you sign up or log in. The account is optional and needs no email or name.</li>
      <li>Sell or share your data. We can't read it, so there's nothing to share.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Hosting</h2>
    <p>
      The sites are hosted by Cloudflare. Like any web host, Cloudflare's servers handle your IP
      address and basic request details (such as which page was asked for and your browser type) to
      deliver pages and protect against attacks. Cloudflare keeps overall counts (like requests per
      day) and a record of requests its firewall blocks. No logs of individual visits are turned on
      for these sites, and nobody looks at that data. See{' '}
      <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare's privacy policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>You're in control</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Each app that saves data has a “Your data” section to back up, restore or delete it all.
        Photos can only delete there: its photos are too big for a backup file, and its copy is its
        online library, with sync on.
      </li>
      <li>
        Backups are files you download. By default they're locked with a passphrase you choose, on
        your device, so only someone with the passphrase can read them. Nobody can recover a
        forgotten passphrase, including us.
      </li>
      <li>You can also clear a site's data in your browser settings.</li>
      <li>
        Unless you turn on sync, your data is only on your device, so clearing your browser data or
        losing the device deletes it. Back up if you want to keep a copy or move to a new device.
      </li>
      <li>
        Safari also deletes a website's data when it isn't opened for 7 days, unless it's on your
        Home Screen. The apps ask your browser to keep their data, but it decides. Adding an app to
        your Home Screen, sync and backups all keep it safe.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      If what leaves your device ever changes, this page is updated first, and the app asks you
      before anything new leaves your device.
    </p>
  </article>
</AppShell>

<style>
  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    border: var(--border-width) solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }

  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  th,
  td {
    padding: var(--space-3);
    text-align: start;
    vertical-align: top;
    border-bottom: var(--border-width) solid var(--color-border);
  }

  tbody tr:last-child > * {
    border-bottom: 0;
  }

  thead th {
    background: var(--color-surface-sunken);
  }

  td ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-inline-start: var(--space-4);
  }
</style>
